1
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:32,300
MATTHEW: Akron, Ohio. The Rubber City. Every summer since the nineteen thirties, kids have come here to roll down a hill in cars they built themselves. No engine. Just gravity. And in 1973, gravity got some help.

2
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:47,739
MATTHEW: I know a little about this. Eighth grade science, Mr. Kobalt's class. We ran a mini pinewood derby. My takeaways were, aerodynamics be darned. Pack that thing with mass and keep the wheels lubed. That is the whole science of a gravity race. There is no step three.

3
00:00:48,039 --> 00:00:54,830
NICK DELUCA: Oh, I know this one. Kid from Colorado stuck a magnet in his car. Cheated, got caught, went home. That is the whole story.

4
00:00:55,130 --> 00:00:58,030
MARCUS VANCE: Nick, that is the first ten seconds of the story.

5
00:00:58,330 --> 00:01:08,840
MATTHEW: August 19, 1973. Derby Downs. A hundred and thirty-eight racers, eleven to fifteen years old. The winner is Jimmy Gronen, fourteen, from Boulder, Colorado.

6
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:13,824
MARCUS VANCE: And people in the stands noticed something. When the steel starting gate dropped, his car seemed to jump.

7
00:01:14,124 --> 00:01:26,428
MATTHEW: Here is how it worked. An electromagnet in the nose of the car. A battery in the tail. And a switch behind his head. He leans his helmet back, the magnet grabs the steel starting gate, and when the gate falls forward it pulls the car with it.

8
00:01:26,728 --> 00:01:29,810
NICK DELUCA: That is actually pretty slick. For a fourteen-year-old.

9
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:33,954
MATTHEW: And that is the tell, Nick. A fourteen-year-old did not do that.

10
00:01:34,254 --> 00:01:39,301
MARCUS VANCE: Officials X-rayed the car. Two days after the race, they took the title away.

11
00:01:39,601 --> 00:01:53,706
MATTHEW: The car was built under the eye of his uncle and legal guardian, Robert Lange. Lange made his fortune in ski boots. The rules said a kid builds his own car and spends no more than seventy-five dollars. Lange reportedly spent about twenty-two thousand.

12
00:01:54,006 --> 00:01:56,546
NICK DELUCA: Twenty-two thousand dollars. On a soap box.

13
00:01:56,846 --> 00:02:08,925
MATTHEW: Then the uncle wrote a letter to the Derby. He said the magnet trick had been around for years. He said the rules were consistently and notoriously violated. And he said it was common knowledge that no eleven-year-old could build a car that wins.

14
00:02:09,225 --> 00:02:14,890
MARCUS VANCE: And he was not wrong about that part. Investigators later found at least thirty Derby cars with illegal axles.

15
00:02:15,190 --> 00:02:17,037
NICK DELUCA: So the whole hill was dirty.

16
00:02:17,337 --> 00:02:25,873
MATTHEW: The whole hill was grown-ups. Engineers. Dads with machine shops. Professional builders. The kids were the drivers, and the kids were the cover story.

17
00:02:26,173 --> 00:02:39,401
MATTHEW: And look who built the stage. For almost forty years the Derby belonged to Chevrolet. General Motors put a boy and a Chevy bowtie in every newspaper in America. Then in 1972 they decided it was not worth the money, and they walked.

18
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:46,168
MARCUS VANCE: Akron was left holding the hill. The Chamber of Commerce ran it in seventy-three. After the magnet, they walked too.

19
00:02:46,468 --> 00:02:52,970
MATTHEW: The county prosecutor here said it was like seeing apple pie, motherhood and the American flag grinding to a halt.

20
00:02:53,270 --> 00:02:56,024
NICK DELUCA: Come on. It is a magnet in a gravity car.

21
00:02:56,324 --> 00:03:06,325
MATTHEW: That is my point, Nick. So count up who paid. The uncle gave two thousand dollars to a boys' club, said he was sorry, and sat out two years. General Motors was already gone.

22
00:03:06,626 --> 00:03:14,337
MATTHEW: And the fourteen-year-old lost the title, lost a seventy-five hundred dollar scholarship, and got his name in every paper in the country next to the word cheater.

23
00:03:14,637 --> 00:03:18,593
MARCUS VANCE: Years later he said one thing about it. The derby was corrupt before we arrived.

24
00:03:18,893 --> 00:03:26,994
MATTHEW: The family of the boy who was handed the trophy said, I feel terrible for the kid. That is more grace than any grown-up on that hill showed all summer.

25
00:03:27,294 --> 00:03:31,468
NICK DELUCA: All right. I take it back. The kid drove the car. Somebody else built the lie.

26
00:03:31,768 --> 00:03:45,165
MATTHEW: A grown man spends twenty-two thousand dollars to win a children's race. A car company uses children to sell cars and leaves when the bill comes. And the only one whose name gets ruined is fourteen. So ask yourself again. Who is the real cheater?

27
00:03:45,465 --> 00:03:50,928
MATTHEW: The Derby still runs every summer, right here. The kids still build the cars. Mostly.

28
00:03:51,228 --> 00:03:53,477
MATTHEW: Akron, Ohio. Keep it real.
